  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "## 12.4.2\n\n### Behavioural changes\n- `\\deff` is read as the document's default font (`DocumentSnapshot.Root.TextElementFormatting`), so a loaded file shows its unformatted text in the font it declares instead of the editor's. The writer makes the document's own font the `\\deff` font. A document without one gets Arial there, marked with `{\\*\\acdnodeff}` under `RoundTripStrict` so it reads back without a default font. The writer used to pick the first font the document happened to use, so another reader showed every unformatted paragraph in it.\n- Font sizes are read and written in points, as Word and the DOCX serializer do. `\\fsN` is N half-points, and a point is 96/72 DIPs, so `\\fs22` (11 pt) loads as a font size of 14.67 and a 14.67 run saves as `\\fs22`. The reader took N/2 as the size in DIPs, so RTF text rendered and exported 25% smaller than the same text from Word or from a DOCX file, and wrapped at different words. Every RTF document now loads with larger text than before; a file saved by an earlier version reads back 4/3 as large, since its sizes were written in the old unit. A size that is not a whole number of half-points rounds to the nearest one on save (it truncated).\n- `\\fs24` is a size of 12 pt (16 DIPs) like any other `\\fsN`. It was read as \"no size\", so the 12 pt text of a Word file took the host's font size instead. A run without `\\fs`, or after `\\plain`, still has no size, and the writer returns a run to no size with `\\plain` (re-emitting the run's other character formatting) rather than `\\fs24`.\n\n### Fixes\n- A font or text color set on a paragraph, a section, a list item, a table cell or the document is listed in the font and color tables. The runs referenced it by an index the tables never declared (`\\f1` with only `\\f0` in `\\fonttbl`), so it was lost on reading. Fonts and colors in a footnote body had the same problem.\n- The font, size, weight, style and text color of a section, list item or table cell reach its text. RTF has no character formatting on a container, and only a paragraph's own formatting was written onto its runs, so the rest was dropped. The document's size and color are written onto the runs the same way and read back as run formatting. A container's background stays its shading and is not written as a highlight.\n- A run without a font that follows one with a font in the same paragraph is shown in the default font again. The writer wrote nothing for the change, so a reader kept the previous run's font; it now writes `\\plain` and the run's remaining character formatting.",
